            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

We identified that your house has a higher than expected amount lot of air leakage that includes the following location:



** The chase for the chimney that goes up from the basement &amp; through the 2nd floor ceiling. We could feel (from the mechanical room ceiling) the air coming down around the outside of the chimney. Using the Infrared Camera, we could see the colder air coming down through the chimney chase from various locations - the corner wall at the top of the stairs in your master bedroom, the chimney chase when looking at it from the main floor family room &amp; from the main floor bathroom ceiling - the joist cavity from the chimney chase all the way through to where the bathroom exhaust fan exits the front of the house.



** The attic hatch in the main floor bedroom that does not seal well - make sure the hatch can come all the way down &amp; you can seal it with a removable caulking such as Draft Attack



** The bathroom exhaust fans are older and do not have good dampers in them - they should be replaced with new Energy Star rated units.



Savings: I modeled a modest 30% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 614 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 43.7 GJ.



There are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction, unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a 30% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 1,000 rebate.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x4@16" (R12), with interior 2x2@24" horizontal strapping with an additional R8 . As per our discussions, you may consider an exterior wall siding &amp; insulation upgrade. This would be a great time to add another minimum R10 of exterior wall insulation (behind the new siding).



I have modeled the addition of a minimum R10 (eg. 2" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the above foundation, exterior main &amp; second floor walls.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 247 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 17.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose to add a minimum R15 or R20, both your rebate &amp; energy savings will increase.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</mainWalls>
                <windows>

Your house has a lot of windows that were top of the line units when the house was originally built. They are still in pretty good shape and my glass analyzer could still detect a Low E coating on the glass !!



As discussed, many homeowners will upgrade windows at the same time they complete an exterior siding upgrade &amp; one of your intentions for having this energy evaluation completed was to identify the expected energy &amp; cost savings.



To help you with this analyses, I have modeled an upgrade to all of the windows to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing all of the windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 275 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates (up to 12 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for window upgrades.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 litre sidewall vented oil hot water tank. As discussed, you may consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a Uniform Energy Factor of 0.91 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current oil hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing propane-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.91 UEF, could potentially save $ 83 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a Max Caddy wood-oil combination furnace. I looked up the specs on this furnace and it is listed as an average delivered efficiency (using wood) of 64.8% and the efficiency for oil is stated as 85%. I could not identify any Canadian test results from Natural Resources Canada, so I used the efficiencies stated by the manufacturer.



You had indicated that you are using this furnace with wood as your primary fuel source.



As discussed during my visit to your house, the EnerGuide Home Energy Evaluation is a "fuel neutral" analyses - it is designed to reduce total energy use (in gigajoules) and this in accomplished by focusing on energy conservation &amp; efficiency of the building envelope &amp; the mechanical equipment inside the house.



From our most recent Fuel Cost Library survey, the December, 2020 fuel cost rates that we use for our Hay River client energy analyses are listed in the "Additional Comments" section of this report. This includes the cost of propane (tank delivery), propane gas (piped to the house), oil (tank delivery) and various biomass fuels. For wood, our December, 2020 survey indicated cord wood was being purchased in Hay River for $300+GST. Even for homeowners who self-harvest, there is a cost associated (gas, chainsaw maintenance, truck &amp; trailer use &amp; maintenance, beer, etc.) so we allocated a nominal $100 per cord for this.



Based on an average delivered efficiency of 64.8% for heating with wood using the Max Caddy, there are obviously more efficient furnaces available .......... that would reduce the gigajoules of energy input required for the heating of your house. As discussed, I will model both efficiency gains as well as estimated impact on cost.



As you have piped propane right to your house, I modeled the installation of an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, this is calculated to decrease your annual energy usage by a whopping 93.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



The estimated cost difference is outlined as follows:

1. Using the $300+GST purchased cost of wood - it would cost a little bit more per year - about $145

2, Using the $100/cord self-harvested rate, the additional yearly cost would be about $2,300



I'm sure the analyses above is not a huge surprise to you ....... and likely why you chose that wood/oil combo furnace and have gone to all the effort to harvest the wood, keep the fire going &amp; haul out the ash - definitely a daily commitment that you work into your schedule. Obviously your time for all of this effort has value, however I do not include that in my calculations - but definitely something for you to think about as you consider the next 20 years of heating your home.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and the one in your master bedroom is an older unit that use a lot of water &amp; should be replaced with a lower water consumption models. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $14,000 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the main floor wall insulation

     - $3,000 for replacing a minimum 12 windows with new eligible units

     - $1,000 for a 30%+ air leakage reduction

     - $3,000 for an eligible furnace upgrade

     - $4,000 Bonus if you achieve a 50% total energy reduction (highly depends upon the furnace upgrade)



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the furnace upgrade, however this would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - on-demand oil hot water heater - $750

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see section further down.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



VENTILATION



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - 2 bathroom fans

        - A clothes dryer



I would recommend you replace the bathroom fans with a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated unit. You may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (2 x bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of only 1.0 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current wood-oil combo furnace and the oil hot water tank each vent out their own chimney, and I do not recall seeing a dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe being located in the mechanical room (for the furnace &amp; hot water tank to use for combustion).



I did see a 5" fresh air supply located at the back of the house that I think connects to the return air ductwork of the furnace. The fresh air supply is meant to bring in &amp; distribute (via the furnace ductwork) outside fresh air each time the furnace operates (or to bring in outside "makeup air" each time an exhaust fan (bathroom fan or dryer) is sucking air out of the house. The fresh air pipe is not intended to also be the source of combustion air for the furnace &amp; hot water tank.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $0.80 per litre 

Propane - $2.46 per m3 for the piped system; or $0.66 per litre delivered 

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.20 per bag, $268.12 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



